meta {
  name: Search the catalog
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}{{apiPrefix}}/game-catalog?q=hollow knight&page=1
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

params:query {
  q: hollow knight
  page: 1
}

docs {
  The add-game picker's search. A server-side proxy over IGDB rather than a browser
  call, because IGDB authenticates through Twitch OAuth and those credentials must
  never reach a client.

  The URL says `game-catalog`, not `igdb`, on purpose: IGDB is the supplier behind
  this route, not the resource it serves, and a path naming the vendor would have
  to change the day a second source is added. The vendor name stays in
  `services/igdb.py` and `repositories/igdb.py`, which genuinely are IGDB clients.
  (It was `GET /igdb/search` until 2026-08-18.)

  A query on a collection rather than a `/search` verb. `q` is required, because
  the collection is IGDB's and cannot be enumerated.

  Authenticated but not owner-scoped: searching costs IGDB quota, not library
  state, so any signed-in user may do it. Rate-limited on its own bucket, and
  `page` is capped so paging cannot be used to grind the quota.

  `hasMore` is the server's answer rather than something the picker infers from
  page length, because only this side knows the cap and knows when a page came
  from a fallback query that cannot be paged at all.

  A GET that WRITES (the Twitch token cache and the rate-limit counters), which is
  why it is blocked on preview deploys. An accepted deviation from GET being safe:
  a prefetching client can burn a little budget, and nothing worse.

  - 200 `{"results": [...], "hasMore": bool}`
  - 401 not logged in
  - 429 over the per-minute search budget
  - 502 Twitch or IGDB upstream failure
  - 503 credentials not configured in this environment
}
